<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Sweden-Norway</h1><p class="st-meta">1814 CE – 1905 CE · capital Stockholm</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Personal union of Sweden and Norway under the Bernadotte kings, created in 1814 and peacefully dissolved by Norway in 1905.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> in 1814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, but the Norwegians adopted a constitution at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eidsvoll">Eidsvoll</a> and elected their own king. After a short war in the summer of 1814, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-moss">Convention of Moss</a> allowed Norway to keep its constitution, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xiii-of-sweden">Charles XIII of Sweden</a> as king of Norway. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-union-1815">Act of Union</a> of 1815 set the terms.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each kingdom kept its own parliament, government, laws, army and currency, sharing only the monarch and foreign policy, which was run from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bernadotte">Bernadotte</a> kings, starting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xiv-john">Charles XIV John</a>, tried to strengthen the union, while Norwegian nationalism and parliamentary democracy grew. In 1884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> won parliamentary government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A dispute over a separate Norwegian consular service came to a head when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> declared on 7 June 1905 that the king had ceased to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Norwegians voted overwhelmingly for dissolution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlstad-convention">Karlstad Convention</a> settled terms peacefully, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-vii">Prince Carl of Denmark</a> became Haakon VII of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sweden-norway.jpg" alt="Sweden-Norway" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Jeltz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swedish_civil_ensign_(1844%E2%80%931905).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1814 CE – 1905 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stockholm</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles XIV John, Oscar I, Oscar II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swedish, Norwegian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United Kingdoms of Sweden and Norway, Union between Sweden and Norway</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=sweden-norway" aria-label="Sweden-Norway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 184 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sweden-Norway lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5381 440L5225 425M5381 440L5381 440M5381 440L5274 535M5381 440L5224 425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0M5381 440h0M5274 535h0M5224 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 440h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Stockholm. <a href="/atlas?year=1860&amp;focus=sweden-norway">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
